          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have coronary artery disease.",
          "answer": "Thank you for sharing that. Coronary artery disease is a condition where plaque, a fatty, cholesterol-rich build-up, forms inside the arteries that supply blood to the heart muscle. According to the General clinical reference (educational summary, \"Coronary artery disease\"), plaque builds up over decades. It can cause symptoms in two main ways:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing** can limit blood flow when the heart needs more oxygen, often causing predictable chest discomfort during activity.\n- **Plaque rupture** can trigger a sudden clot that blocks the artery. This can happen even at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms.\n\nIf you have chest pain, pressure, or breathlessness that is new, getting worse, or occurs at rest, call your local emergency number right away.\n\nWhat would you like to know? I can cover:\n- Symptoms to watch for\n- Lifestyle guidance (diet, activity, sleep)\n- How the condition is classified in ICD-10 coding\n- General information on how it is managed\n\nFor treatment decisions, your clinician is the right person to talk to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
