        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T03:07:55.355413Z",
          "trace_id": "5bab3938ad9b4919b2b88f1e0e8415e6",
          "session_id": "full-mt-m-015",
          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is meant by secondary prevention?",
          "answer": "Secondary prevention is the set of measures used for someone who already has an established disease, to reduce the chance of further problems. For coronary artery disease, the guidance defines it as the measures used after a previous heart attack, a procedure to open the arteries, or disease seen on a heart scan (angiography). It is aimed at stopping the disease from getting worse and causing another event. (Source: Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention after a diagnosis, major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary.)\n\nThe guidance describes it as a package, not a single treatment. Its main parts include:\n\n- **Antiplatelet medicine**, which helps prevent clots. The guidance says the length of treatment is tailored to bleeding risk.\n- **High-intensity statin therapy**, a cholesterol-lowering treatment. Lipid guidance lists this as the recommended approach for established heart disease. (Source: Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation.)\n- **Non-drug measures**, which the guidance says are the parts most often dropped. It does not list them in the passage I retrieved.\n\nYour clinician can tell you which of these apply to you.\n\nI could not find the general definitions of primary and tertiary prevention in the reference material I can search, so I can't give a sourced contrast. In outline, primary prevention aims to stop a disease from starting, and tertiary prevention aims to reduce the impact of a disease that is already long-term. Please check a public health or medical education source for those definitions.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 7912.196626999503,
          "red_flag_matched": false,
